<<simex1_tab,echo=FALSE>>=
options(knitr.kable.NA = '.',format="latex")
tab_ex1<-kable(resf,longtable=FALSE,align='c',format="latex",booktabs=TRUE,escape=F,digits=3,
caption="\\label{tab:simex1} Simulated data example Cox hazard ratio (hr) estimates for the ITT population and subgroups $H$ and $H^{c}$.
Cox model estimates are based on subgroups: H true (knowing the actual subgroup, a-priori); the estimated subgroup $\\hat{H}$; and 
the boostrap bias-correction to $\\hat{H}$ estimates, denoted $\\hat{H}_{bc}$.  Estimates for the complement $H^{c}$ are defined analogously.") %>%
kable_styling(full_width = FALSE, font_size=9,latex_options="hold_position") %>%
group_rows("ITT", 1, 1) %>%
group_rows("H subgroup estimates", 2, 4) %>%
group_rows("H-complement subgroup estimates", 5, 7)
@


As a practical illustration we consider a simulated dataset generated as described in Section \ref{sec:sims}.   In this simulated example there are 
$n=\Sexpr{length(df$y.sim)}$ subjects, randomized 1:1, where the underlying (marginal) hazard ratio for the harm population $H$ is 
$\hplim =\Sexpr{round(dgm$hr.H.true,2)}$, and for the complement $H^{c}$, the (marginal) hazard ratio is $\hcplim =\Sexpr{round(dgm$hr.Hc.true,2)}$.  The overall (marginal) hazard ratio is $\Sexpr{round(dgm$hr.causal,2)}$.  The underlying proportion of subjects in the harm population $H$ is $\approx \Sexpr{pH_super}\%$.  Here the actual number of subjects in the $H$ subgroup is $\Sexpr{sum(x$flag.harm)}$ and the number of subjects in the complement equals $\Sexpr{sum(1-x$flag.harm)}$.  

Applying our novel procedure the estimated subgroup $\hat{H}$ consists of $\Sexpr{nH_est}$ subjects and thus 
$\Sexpr{nn-nH_est}$ in the estimated complement $\hat{H}^{c}$. The number of subjects in $H$ that are included in $\hat{H}$ is $\Sexpr{kH}$ (i.e., $|H \cap \hat{H}|=\Sexpr{kH}$): Of the $\Sexpr{nH_true}$ subjects in $H$, $\Sexpr{kH}/\Sexpr{nH_true}=\Sexpr{100*round(kH/nH_true,2)}\%$ are correctly classified and of the $\Sexpr{nH_est}$ subjects in $\hat{H}$, $\Sexpr{kH}/\Sexpr{nH_est}=\Sexpr{100*round(kH/nH_est,2)}\%$ are correctly classified.  For the complement, the number of subjects in $H^{c}$ that are included in $\hat{H}^{c}$ is $\Sexpr{kHc}$: Of the $\Sexpr{nHc_true}$ subjects in $H^{c}$, $\Sexpr{kHc}/\Sexpr{nHc_true}=\Sexpr{100*round(kHc/nHc_true,2)}\%$ are correctly classified and of the $\Sexpr{nHc_est}$ subjects in $\hat{H}^{c}$, $\Sexpr{kHc}/\Sexpr{nHc_est}=\Sexpr{100*round(kHc/nHc_est,2)}\%$ are correctly classified.  In the sequel we refer to these as specificity and sensitivity metrics for $\hat{H}$, and $\hat{H}^{c}$.


\Sexpr{tab_ex1}


Figure \ref{fig:simex1} displays the Kaplan-Meier curves based on the true (known a-priori) subgroups and the estimated subgroups (Figures \ref{fig:simex1}(a) and \ref{fig:simex1}(b), for $H$ and $H^{c}$, respectively).  The Kaplan-Meier curves for the treatment and control arms for the actual subgroups ($H$, known a-prior) are generally well characterized by the estimated subgroups.  
Table \ref{tab:simex1} provides the Cox ITT estimate for the overall population as well as the estimates based on the true (known a-priori), and estimated subgroups, $\hat{H}$ and $\hat{H}^{c}$.  That the ITT estimate is 
not significant highlights the practical implications.  For characterizing $H$, recall that the underlying (marginal) hazard ratio is $\hplim =\Sexpr{round(dgm$hr.H.true,2)}$.  If the true H is known (a-priori), the Cox estimate is $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[1,1]),2)}$ (95\% CI=$\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[1,2]),2)}$, $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[1,3]),2)}$), and for the estimated H, $\hat{H}$, the Cox estimate is $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[2,1]),2)}$ (95\% CI=$\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[2,2]),2)}$, 
$\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[2,3]),2)}$).  Similar to the model selection area, we expect Cox model estimates based on an estimated subgroup to be optimistic.  We therefore describe a bootstrap bias-correction, 
which in this case, the bias-corrected Cox estimate (denoted by $\hat{H}_{bc}$) is $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[3,1]),2)}$ (95\% CI=$\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[3,2]),2)}$, $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[3,3]),2)}$) which we see provides a more ``tempered''estimate and is quite similar to the estimates based on the ideal true H in this simulated example.  
In addition, notice that the CIs for each estimate cover the underlying (marginal) respective hazard ratios 
$\hplim =\Sexpr{round(dgm$hr.H.true,2)}$ and $\hcplim =\Sexpr{round(dgm$hr.Hc.true,2)}$ for $H$ and $H^{c}$, respectively (coverage indicator is denoted C(fixed) in Table \ref{tab:simex1}).  
The CIs for $\hat{H}$ and $\hat{H}_{bc}$ also cover the estimate based on $H$ true =  $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resH[1,1]),2)}$; and similarly for the complement where $H^{c}$ true =  $\Sexpr{round(as.numeric(resHc[1,1]),2)}$ (coverage indicator is denoted C(true)).  We remark that this simulated example is representative of the results observed in our simulation setting described in Section \ref{sec:sims}.



